I found the portal to Fort Ludios on level 13 or so and thought to
myself, "I can do this!"  I was playing an Orcish Rogue - I'd finished
Mines' End and Sokoban, was down to AC:-15 (GDSM from a magic lamp,
protection from Kos and the cross-aligned Gnometown priestess).  On the
level with the portal to Ludios there was an altar, where I'd made my
first sacrifices of the game and received Stormbringer in return.  By
the time I'd made it through all of the soldiers, sergeants,
lieutenants and captains that come at you at through that second door,
I'd worked my way up to Skilled in both broadsword and two-weapon
(weilding both Stormy and a +5 dagger I'd thankfully found early in the
game).  I wasn't even bothering to use my stack of daggers because I
was kicking so much ass in melee.  Aside from a brief brush with an
energy vortex, nothing was denting my hit points more than 20 or so
from my max (90), not even the handful of dragons I'd made short work
of.

Basically it was the best character I'd ever played that wasn't a
Wizard or a Valkyrie.  I was feeling pretty good about myself.  O,
Hubris!  O, Pride!  I should have known my fall was imminent.

As I entered the throne room, I was mostly concerned about the number
of peaceful monsters I'd been killing - thanks to my bloodthirsty blade
- so I was trying to be a bit more careful.  Okay, little 'o', you can
go.  Some of those 'C's I killed earlier were peaceful, but these three
mountain centaurs certainly aren't, since they're wailing on my
something fierce.  What's that purple @ over there?  Must be an
Elvenking ... couldn't possibly be peaceful, but shouldn't be a
problem, I'll worry about him in a second.

Then I woke up my neighbors with a gasp of stupefied disbelief,
followed shortly by a string of unholy curses.  "Croesus hits with his
two-handed sword!  You die..."

I had forgotten about the very existence of Croesus!  I just plumb
forgot about the damned king of Lydia.

The real irony is that I had a million ways of dealing with him ... a
mean stack of enchanted daggers, for instance.  A wand of death, for
heaven's sake.  I had been sure that Magnificent the Rogue was
ascension-bound, and he might have been, had I stopped to think for a
second about what waited beyond those god-forsaken doors.

